Let LUA_ROOT be set from the build, instead of always /usr/local/.

luaconf.h defines LUA_ROOT unconditionally and builds LUA_PATH_DEFAULT and
LUA_CPATH_DEFAULT out of it, so a -DLUA_ROOT on the command line is quietly
overridden by the header -- the value compiled into the interpreter is always
/usr/local/, whatever the package was configured with.

This port installs Lua under /usr (INSTALL_TOP=$SYSROOT/usr), so require()
searched /usr/local/lib/lua/5.1/ for C modules while LuaFileSystem was
installed in /usr/lib/lua/5.1/.  luakit stopped on its first line:

    Lua error: module 'lfs' not found
      no file '/usr/local/lib/lua/5.1/lfs.so'
      ...

listing a dozen candidates, none of them where the file is.  Note the .lua
list looks longer than the .so list only because luakit appends its own
directories to package.path; nothing appends to package.cpath, so a C module
is found through this default or not at all.

Guarding the definition is the whole change -- the fallback is upstream's own
value, so a build that says nothing still gets /usr/local/ and behaves exactly
as before.  LUA_LDIR and LUA_CDIR are left alone: they derive from LUA_ROOT,
so they follow it.

Upstream: not reported (Lua 5.1 is long out of maintenance; 5.2 onwards
already guard this).

--- a/src/luaconf.h
+++ b/src/luaconf.h
@@ -94,7 +94,9 @@
 	".\\?.dll;"  LUA_CDIR"?.dll;" LUA_CDIR"loadall.dll"
 
 #else
+#ifndef LUA_ROOT
 #define LUA_ROOT	"/usr/local/"
+#endif
 #define LUA_LDIR	LUA_ROOT "share/lua/5.1/"
 #define LUA_CDIR	LUA_ROOT "lib/lua/5.1/"
 #define LUA_PATH_DEFAULT  \
